# Check every text file out with LF line endings, on every host.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which converts text files
# to CRLF in the working tree. Several things in this repository read their
# own sources byte for byte and break on a CRLF checkout:
#
# * tests/boundary/vectors/SHA256SUMS is split on "\n", so every file name
#   it lists keeps a trailing "\r" and cannot be opened.
# * The boundary-shim falsifier tests (tests/boundary/fetch-*.test.ts) rewrite
#   one exact anchor in the shipped source before bundling it, and some
#   anchors span a line break; with CRLF the anchor no longer occurs and the
#   inverse is never applied.
# * Shell scripts run by `just` and the Nix files are read by tools that do
#   not accept "\r".
#
# `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` (https://git-scm.com/docs/gitattributes,
# "eol"), so the working tree is the same bytes as the repository on Linux,
# macOS and Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary test vectors: never converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto`
# would already detect most of them, but a short vector can look like text to
# git's heuristic (it only checks for NUL bytes), so they are named here.
*.bin binary
